"A domain aggregate diagram shows the entity boundary that protects invariants and the root through which changes are made.\n\nUse when: Review transaction scope and consistency rules. Explain which references and behaviors belong to an aggregate root.\n\nAvoid when: The objective is a full persistence schema.\n\nVisual grammar: A padded enclosure denotes one aggregate boundary. The root receives a clear role label; internal entities and value objects remain distinct. External references use identities and labeled relationships across boundaries.\n\nBuild: Start with invariants that must hold after a transaction. Choose the root responsible for enforcing those invariants. Place owned entities and value objects inside its boundary. Attach behavior to the owning entity and route external identity references outside internal labels.\n\nCheck: External modifications enter through the aggregate root. The boundary follows consistency needs rather than table joins. Cross-aggregate assumptions are explicit and proposals are labeled. The root and boundary title are readable without intersecting connectors.\n\nPitfalls: Making every related entity part of one oversized aggregate. Equating an aggregate with any object graph returned by an ORM.\n\nExample: Order owns OrderLines and enforces its total; Customer is referenced by identity across a separate boundary.\n\nAlternatives: Class diagram, Bounded context map."
